  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe core idea is to construct a structured initial guess based on a periodic function with controlled overlap properties, then refine it using a hybrid optimization strategy that combines global exploration with local search. This approach addresses the limitations of gradient-based methods by starting with a candidate that inherently balances the integral constraint and minimizes overlap, then applying a more robust optimization process to fine-tune the solution. The key innovation is using a piecewise constant function with pre-defined periodicity that reduces the worst-case correlation, avoiding local minima that standard methods may get stuck in.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe represent h as a piecewise constant function with n_points samples over [0, 2], satisfying:\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n- \u2211h[i] = n_points / 2\n\nThe objective is to minimize:\nC\u2085 = max_k \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1 - h(x + k)) dx\n\nThis is equivalent to finding the maximum value of:\nC\u2085 = max_lag (np.correlate(h, 1 - h, mode=\"full\") * dx)\n\nConstraints:\n- h \u2208 [0, 1]\u207f_points\n- \u2211h = n_points / 2\n\nWe formulate this as a constrained optimization problem:\nminimize  max_lag (correlation(lag) * dx)\nsubject to:\n- \u2211h = n_points / 2\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n\nWe use a surrogate function with a smooth approximation of the max_lag term to enable gradient-based optimization, and validate the results with the exact evaluator.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nStage 1: Parent validation and initialization\n- If initial_h_values is provided, verify sum(h) \u2248 n_points / 2 (tolerance 1e-6)\n- If no parent, create a periodic step function with period T:\n   - T = 2 / m, where m is an integer divisor of n_points\n   - Generate h[i] = 1 if i mod m < m/2, else 0\n   - Rescale to ensure \u2211h = n_points / 2\n\nStage 2: Global search with phase-space perturbation\n- For 100 iterations:\n   - Apply random phase rotation to the periodic pattern\n   - Add small sinusoidal perturbations to avoid symmetry\n   - Clip to [0, 1] and re-normalize \u2211h\n   - Evaluate C\u2085 using the exact correlation function\n\nStage 3: Local refinement with multi-stage L-BFGS-B\n- Use the best candidate from Stage 2 as initial guess\n- Perform 200 iterations of L-BFGS-B optimization:\n   - Objective: -C\u2085 (minimize negative to maximize)\n   - Bounds: [0, 1] for all elements\n   - Constraint: \u2211h = n_points / 2 (equality constraint)\n- Track the best solution during optimization\n- Apply adaptive step size scaling based on gradient magnitude\n\nStage 4: Final evaluation with exact metric\n- Re-evaluate the best solution using the exact correlation function\n- Cross-check with a fresh minimize call\n- Ensure all constraints are satisfied within 1e-6 tolerance\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nDefault configuration:\n- n_points = 600 (provides better resolution than 400)\n- Periodic T = 0.0167 (corresponds to m = 120)\n- Phase rotation range: \u00b1\u03c0/4\n- Perturbation frequency: 10 Hz (approx 2\u03c0/T)\n- Initial random seed = 42\n\nVariations:\n- If parent exists, apply a 5% sinusoidal perturbation to the initial guess\n- For n_points < 200, interleave with a higher frequency pattern\n- If convergence stalls, switch to differential evolution for exploration\n- Use a smoothed version of max_lag for early optimization stages\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nTime allocation:\n- 150s for global search with phase-space exploration\n- 350s for local refinement with L-BFGS-B\n- 100s for final evaluation and fallback\n\nRobustness measures:\n- Clamp all values to [0, 1] after each update\n- If \u2211h deviates by >1e-5, apply a rescaling factor\n- Track top 5 candidates and re-evaluate them if optimization stalls\n- Use early stopping if C\u2085 improves by <1e-5 over 10 iterations\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFeasibility checks:\n- Verify all h[i] \u2208 [0, 1] within 1e-6\n- Confirm \u2211h == n_points / 2 within 1e-6\n- Ensure C\u2085 is a finite positive number\n\nFinal validation:\n- Re-evaluate the candidate using the exact correlation function\n- Cross-check with a fresh minimize call\n- Output the list of h_values, C\u2085 bound, and n_points\n\nSanity checks:\n- Confirm no NaNs or infinities in the result\n- Validate that the correlation calculation matches the evaluator\n- Ensure the return shape is exactly (n_points,) for h_values",
